The subset holds no unsafe, and the Dart output holds 1 cell of a list for each
element of a slice. The diff removes 3 tests for those 2 causes, and it keeps
each other line of the file.

TestIssue68488 reads the machine address of the storage of a slice with
unsafe.SliceData. The test measures a liveness rule of the garbage collector of
Go: a clone of a slice of length 0 must hold no pointer into the array of the
source. The Dart output can express no such rule. A slice of the runtime holds
a list, an offset, a length, and a capacity, and an append above the capacity
always copies, thus no program can read the difference. TestClone and
TestNilness cover each value rule of Clone.

TestConcat_too_large and the big slices of TestRepeat build a slice of about 2
to the power 63 elements of a type of no size. Go gives such an allocation 1
address and no storage, thus the length costs nothing there. A cell of a list
of Dart costs a word for each element, also for a type of no size, and the
bound $maxSliceLen of the runtime stands at 2 to the power 45. The make of the
operand thus panics before Concat or Repeat runs. TestConcat and the normal
cases of TestRepeat cover each value rule of the 2 functions, and
TestRepeatPanics still proves the overflow rule of Repeat. docs/parity.md holds
the bound.

